---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
import { escalations, scorecards, stats } from "../lib/data";
import { dec, n, pct } from "../lib/format";

const m = scorecards.maintainer_labels;
const o = scorecards.operator_labels;
const ask = scorecards.ask;
const runs = scorecards.runs;
const reject = runs["why_1.2_was_rejected"];
const frames = escalations.filter((e: any) => String(e.label).startsWith("[<")).length;
const lineRuns = runs.bgl_line_metric_across_runs.runs.map((r: any) => r.suppression);
const lowRun = Math.min(...lineRuns);
const defect = m.per_category.defect;
const feature = m.per_category.feature_request;
---
<Site
  title="How it was measured"
  description="Agreement with the repositories' own maintainers, recall against a supercomputer's operator labels, a prompt revision that scored better and was rejected, and the failures."
  current="measured"
>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <header class="head">
      <h1>How it was measured</h1>
      <p class="lede">Every result here is checked against labels that nobody working on this project chose. The method comes first, then the number, then what it does and does not show.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="result">
      <div class="q">
        <h2>Does it agree with the people who maintain these repositories?</h2>
        <p class="method">Method: {n(m.pairs)} GitHub issues from transformers, next.js, pytorch, langchain, vscode and kubernetes, each already labelled by that repository's own maintainers (bug, feature and similar). Its labels were compared with theirs, without either side seeing the other.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fig">
        <p class="big">{dec(m.cohens_kappa, 3)}</p>
        <p class="what">Cohen's kappa, 95% interval {dec(m.kappa_ci95[0], 3)} to {dec(m.kappa_ci95[1], 3)}</p>
        <p>Raw agreement {pct(m.raw_agreement)}. Kappa corrects that for the agreement two raters would reach by chance; 0.6 to 0.8 is usually called substantial.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Precision</th><th>Recall</th><th>Issues</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>defect</td><td>{dec(defect.precision, 2)}</td><td>{dec(defect.recall, 2)}</td><td>{n(defect.support)}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>feature request</td><td>{dec(feature.precision, 2)}</td><td>{dec(feature.recall, 2)}</td><td>{n(feature.support)}</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="result">
      <div class="q">
        <h2>Does it surface what the operators flagged?</h2>
        <p class="method">Method: {n(o.lines)} lines of the Blue Gene/L supercomputer log, every line labelled by the operations staff who ran the machine as routine or as an alert. The labels never reach the model. A pile it calls routine is hidden; anything else is shown to a person.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fig">
        <p class="big">{n(o.by_cluster.surfaced)} <span>of {n(o.by_cluster.clusters)}</span></p>
        <p class="what">piles shown to a person, instead of {n(o.lines)} lines</p>
        <p>Every pile that held an operator alert was shown ({n(o.by_cluster.alert_bearing_surfaced)} of {n(o.by_cluster.alert_bearing)}), and all {n(o.alert_surfaced)} flagged lines with them: recall {dec(o.alert_recall, 2)}.</p>
        <p>The line-level figures swing between runs because one pile, a kernel message about core dumps, is most of the log. Operators called it routine; as text it reads like a crash. This run hid it, so {pct(o.noise_suppression)} of routine lines were suppressed; an earlier run showed it and suppressed {pct(lowRun)}. The pile count is the number that holds.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="result">
      <div class="q">
        <h2>A better score, rejected</h2>
        <p class="method">Method: the labelled issues were split in half with a fixed seed. Three rules were written against the disagreements in one half, and the other half decided whether they helped.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fig">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Prompt</th><th>Kappa on the held-out half, per run</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1.1, in use</td><td>{runs["prompt_1.1"].map((r: any) => dec(r.kappa, 3)).join(", ")}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1.2, revised</td><td>{runs["prompt_1.2"].map((r: any) => dec(r.kappa, 3)).join(", ")}</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>The revision won every run. It was still rejected, because of what it cost elsewhere:</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>1.1</th><th>1.2</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {reject.rows.map((r: any) => <tr><td>{r.measure}</td><td>{r["prompt_1.1"]}</td><td>{r["prompt_1.2"]}</td></tr>)}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="decided">{reject.decided_by}</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="result">
      <div class="q">
        <h2>Can you ask it questions?</h2>
        <p class="method">Method: {n(ask.questions)} questions written before the search was built and never edited, such as "when did the SSH password failures start". A hit means the right pile came back first.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fig">
        <p class="big">{dec(ask.hit_at_1, 2)}</p>
        <p class="what">right pile first, {dec(ask.hit_at_3, 2)} within the top three</p>
        <p>Counting and timing questions are answered from the database, not by a model. The one question it cannot answer uses words the corpus never does: <q>{ask.misses[0]}</q>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="result fails">
      <div class="q">
        <h2>Where it falls short</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="fig">
        <li><b>Stack traces split apart.</b> Logs are read a line at a time, so a Linux kernel stack trace becomes one pile per line of the trace. {n(frames)} of the {n(stats.outcomes.person)} piles sent to a person are single frames of kernel stack traces rather than whole problems.</li>
        <li><b>A lost alert type.</b> The supercomputer log was cut to {n(o.lines)} lines by taking every eighth line, without looking at the labels. One alert type appeared on a single line and fell out of the sample. Keeping it would have meant choosing lines by their answers.</li>
        <li><b>The tracker is a sample.</b> The rulebook would file {n(stats.outcomes.ticket)} tickets; the demo Jira holds {n(stats.tickets)}, so as not to flood it.</li>
        <li><b>Runs vary.</b> Free model tiers route each run to a different mix of models, so the same prompt on the same issues has scored anywhere in the ranges above.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</Site>

<style>
  .page { padding-top: var(--space-7); }
  .head { max-width: 60rem; }
  .lede { margin-top: var(--space-4); font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.5; color: var(--soft-ink); max-width: 48ch; }

  .result { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr); gap: var(--space-7); margin-top: var(--space-7); padding-top: var(--space-6); border-top: 2px solid var(--ink); align-items: start; }
  .q { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); }
  .q h2 { font-size: var(--step-2); }
  .method { color: var(--soft-ink); max-width: 52ch; }
  .fig { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); }
  .fig p { max-width: var(--measure); color: var(--soft-ink); }
  .big { font: 900 var(--step-4)/0.95 var(--font-display); letter-spacing: -0.03em; color: var(--ink) !important; }
  .big span { font-size: 0.45em; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0; }
  .what { font: 400 var(--step--1)/1.4 var(--font-mono); color: var(--muted) !important; }
  .decided { padding: var(--space-4); background: var(--panel); color: var(--panel-ink) !important; }

  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--step--1); }
  th { text-align: left; font: 400 var(--step--2)/1.3 var(--font-mono); color: var(--muted); padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3) var(--space-2) 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink); }
  td { padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-3) var(--space-3) 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); vertical-align: top; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  td:not(:first-child) { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.85em; }

  .fails ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .fails li { padding-bottom: var(--space-4); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); color: var(--soft-ink); max-width: var(--measure); }
  .fails b { color: var(--ink); }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .result { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-5); }
  }
</style>
